This page is for **library authors**. If you're building an app, you can skip it — your islands already work.

## The one case that needs it

ogygia's build prescans your app's `src`, so every island you mark — in a page, a hook, a `document()`-served page — gets its client hydrate chunk. There's exactly one blind spot: a **library** whose islands are marked inside its *own* files and rendered only from **server code** (a [`document()`](/docs/regions/held-regions) page, like the profiler's own UI). Those components never enter the app's client module graph, so their chunks never build — and hydration 404s at runtime.

## Declare your island roots

A library that ships such islands lists their directories in its own `package.json`:

```json title=package.json
{
  "name": "my-widgets",
  "ogygia": {
    "islands": ["./src/ui", "./dist/ui"]
  }
}
```

ogygia's plugin walks every direct dependency's manifest and adds the declared dirs as prescan roots — in **both** build legs, so the ids match and the SSR entry URLs line up with the emitted chunks. List both your source and shipped-`dist` dirs: the first one that exists wins (a workspace consuming your `src` uses that; a published install uses `dist` — never both, so no duplicate registration). Paths resolve **inside your package only**.

That's the whole feature. A consuming app installs your library and renders your server-side island UI with zero extra wiring.

::: tip Ordinary component libraries need nothing
If your components are imported and marked by the *consuming app* (the normal case — `import Widget from 'my-widgets' with { wake: 'load' }`), they're already in the app's graph and build fine. The manifest is only for islands your library renders itself, from server code.
:::
